#: 6146185e1d354d18967335702770bdbc
msgid "The following table may be helpful matching your local radio regulations to the two radio models available"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:469
#: 28b31a15320240c1b72142b0a238269a
msgid "DUTY_CYCLE setting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:471
#: 09250135a60c4d4a947827502d19b8ad
msgid "Most users will want to set the ``DUTY_CYCLE`` to 100. The ``DUTY_CYCLE`` is the maximum percentage of time that the radio will transmit packets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:475
#: 7ccfbf92f5fd4349acec6f956a69eb6b
msgid "The reason the duty cycle is included is that some regions of the world allow for higher transmit power or more frequencies if you have a duty cycle below a given threshold. So for example in Europe you can transmit on a wider range of frequencies in the 433 band if your duty cycle is below 10%."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:481
#: dbec6cbd84b74be8879689892f594119
msgid "When you set a duty cycle below 100% then your available bandwidth will be reduced, so you will find it will only work well for telemetry at higher baud rates. It is still quite practical to get good telemetry from ArduPilot with a 10% duty cycle, as telemetry traffic is quite 'bursty', so the average transmit time is not generally high anyway."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:487
#: 8a360dae497c4d328ac7c0c7291b59f7
msgid "For example, you can easily receive all telemetry streams at 2Hz with ``AIR_SPEED`` set to 128, ECC enabled and ``DUTY_CYCLE`` set to 10."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:490
#: f046fe92fd4f4864963947df359754d0
msgid "You can also set a radio to receive only by setting the ``DUTY_CYCLE`` to 0. That will work best if you set ``NUM_CHANNELS`` to a low number, as otherwise the clock synchronisation will be poor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:497
#: b5c5cf1e1ace4ecf9f34e3375ac3a311
msgid "Low latency mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:499
#: 547719cbeea348ebb2b70d416158a9a6
msgid "The radio can be configured to use 'low latency mode' to improve performance for things like tablet based joysticks etc... The two parameters you should set to enable this mode are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:503
#: 098b7a169c254ab9a581961a47c3447a
msgid "Set ``MAVLINK`` to 2. This turns on special checking for the ``RC_OVERRIDE`` packets used in joystick control, to allow those packets to always be sent first. If you are using MAVLink (you probably are) there are no downsides for choosing this setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:507
#: 291a82d1c00a47e6a9f6e6e001d092ce
msgid "Change ``MAX_WINDOW`` from the default of 131 to 33. This will ensure that the GCS can send a packet to the vehicle at least once every 33 msecs. It is worth noting that this will lower the available bandwidth, so if you need absolute maximum bandwidth you are best off with the default of 131. **Both radios on a channel must have the same value for this parameter, or they will not be able to talk to each other.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:516
#: aeb65374d52648ef9fbe2c77037e3184
msgid "Listen Before Talk (LBT)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:518
#: bb0e127362f1495fa7ea88b27351c603
msgid "The radio can implement 'listen before talk' (LBT) functionality to allow it to comply with a wider range of regional regulatory requirements. LBT is a system where the radio is required to listen for a period of time and see no signal from other radios before it is allowed to transmit. By using a non-zero ``LBT_RSSI`` value your radio will become more 'polite', by waiting until everyone else has stopped transmitting before starting to transmit itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:526
#: 6a6e9b61222f4c0fbcb42c3b302a876b
msgid "To enable LBT in your radio you need to set the ``LBT_RSSI`` threshold. This is the signal strength that the radio considers to be an indication that the channel is busy. If you set ``LBT_RSSI`` to zero then LBT is disabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:531
#: 1109a6d9b09945f5a08058a88f91fd53
msgid "The minimum non-zero setting is 25 which is a few dB above the receive sensitivity of the radio (-121 dBm). To setup ``LBT_RSSI`` you need to know what signal level your local radio regulations require for LBT functionality. Each increment in LBT_RSSI above 25 is roughly equal to 0.5dB above the radios receive sensitivity. So if you set LBT_RSSI to 40 then the radio will consider the channel to be free if the signal strength is less than 7.5dB above the receiver sensitivity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:539
#: 436c03ce99ba4b0cad2bc1c3a8801b8a
msgid "Alternatively, you can use this formula to get the received signal strength in dBm:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:546
#: 50513185c49f4d859e59fcd4b67e65ec
msgid "This formula is approximate, but quite close. See the Si1000 data sheet for a more precise graph."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:549
#: f275f3350ec24adf8905e68f05c046ae
msgid "You will need to lookup your local regulatory requirements to see what ``LBT_RSSI`` setting you should use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:552
#: 58496dfaf5a14fce82c711e09bcf1c91
msgid "The LBT implementation in the radio uses a minimum listen time of 5ms, plus randomised listen time as per the European 9.2.2.2 rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:555
#: 521f7bc830d64d2e8c13e5f52ea85d9a
msgid "Note that in many regions you need to implement LBT in conjunction with AFA (Adaptive Frequency Agility). The radio implements AFA as long as you have ``NUM_CHANNELS`` set to more than 1."
msgstr ""

